SYNTHETIC reply written by hand for the ttsim end-to-end check: a module whose inputs hold half the elements of the input files.

```mlir
// FILE: eltwise_binary.mlir
// The deliberately mis-shaped TTIR module of tt-pairs-v0's eltwise_binary for the end-to-end check of
// plans/spikes/ttmlir-runtime.md (2026-10-30): eltwise_binary_ok.mlir on 128x256 tensors, half the 65536 elements of
// each input file, so it compiles and the driver must refuse its inputs before it opens the device. Written by hand
// for the check; never a model's output.
func.func @eltwise_binary(%a: tensor<128x256xbf16>, %b: tensor<128x256xbf16>) -> tensor<128x256xf32> {
  %sum = "ttir.add"(%a, %b) : (tensor<128x256xbf16>, tensor<128x256xbf16>) -> tensor<128x256xbf16>
  %c = "ttir.typecast"(%sum) : (tensor<128x256xbf16>) -> tensor<128x256xf32>
  return %c : tensor<128x256xf32>
}
```

SYNTHETIC reply written by hand for the ttsim end-to-end check: a module that computes a - b.

```mlir
// FILE: eltwise_binary.mlir
// The deliberately wrong TTIR module of tt-pairs-v0's eltwise_binary for the end-to-end check of
// plans/spikes/ttmlir-runtime.md (2026-10-30): eltwise_binary_ok.mlir with ttir.subtract in place of ttir.add, so it
// compiles and runs and its output c must fail the oracle. Written by hand for the check; never a model's output.
func.func @eltwise_binary(%a: tensor<256x256xbf16>, %b: tensor<256x256xbf16>) -> tensor<256x256xf32> {
  %diff = "ttir.subtract"(%a, %b) : (tensor<256x256xbf16>, tensor<256x256xbf16>) -> tensor<256x256xbf16>
  %c = "ttir.typecast"(%diff) : (tensor<256x256xbf16>) -> tensor<256x256xf32>
  return %c : tensor<256x256xf32>
}
```

SYNTHETIC reply written by hand for the ttsim end-to-end check: the correct module, a + b widened to f32.

```mlir
// FILE: eltwise_binary.mlir
// The hand-written correct TTIR module of tt-pairs-v0's eltwise_binary for the end-to-end check of
// plans/spikes/ttmlir-runtime.md (2026-10-30): the two bf16 inputs of 65536 elements written as 256x256 tensors
// (the same elements in row-major order), a bf16 add on the device, and the sum widened to f32, which is what the
// item's tt program writes as its output c. Written by hand for the check; never a model's output.
func.func @eltwise_binary(%a: tensor<256x256xbf16>, %b: tensor<256x256xbf16>) -> tensor<256x256xf32> {
  %sum = "ttir.add"(%a, %b) : (tensor<256x256xbf16>, tensor<256x256xbf16>) -> tensor<256x256xbf16>
  %c = "ttir.typecast"(%sum) : (tensor<256x256xbf16>) -> tensor<256x256xf32>
  return %c : tensor<256x256xf32>
}
```
